// @NAME: Getting Started
// @TITLE: Three verbs over a text — and the pattern that fails without saying so.
//
// `regex` gives a `text` three methods: `search` for where a pattern first matches,
// `matches` for whether it matches at all, and `split_on` for the pieces between matches.
// Patterns are written inline and cached, so calling the same one in a loop is cheap.
//
// It is deliberately the SMALL-SCRIPT tool — log scanning, CLI fields, quick extraction —
// and not a parser. Grammars, ASTs and recursion are somebody else's job, and there is no
// `replace` or capture-group accessor yet.
//
// ⚠ **The verbs are named around the stdlib, not after the usual regex words**, and that is
// the first thing to know. `text` already has `find` (a LITERAL substring search) and
// `split` (on a CHARACTER), and a method wins name resolution over an imported free
// function — so a bare `find(pattern, line)` compiles, type-checks and answers the stdlib's
// literal search instead of this one. Hence `search` and `split_on`. `matches` is
// collision-free and keeps its bare name.
use regex;

fn main() {
  line = "order 1234 shipped 2026-09-09";

  at = line.search("[0-9]+");
  println("first number at byte {at}");
  println("has a 4-digit run : {line.matches(\"[0-9]{4}\")}");

  assert(at == 6, "the first digit run starts at byte 6");
  assert(line.matches("[0-9]{4}"), "1234 is four digits");
  assert(!line.matches("[a-z]{20}"), "and nothing here is twenty letters");

  // Every section below is a function, and `main` calls each one. A section `main` does not
  // call still COMPILES — so a renamed API is caught, and the assertions that check the
  // example is RIGHT never run.
  a_pattern_that_never_compiled();
  the_offset_is_a_byte();
  splitting_yields_the_gaps();
  the_older_spellings_still_work();
}

// That prints:
//
//     first number at byte 6
//     has a 4-digit run : true
//
// ## The one that bites: an invalid pattern answers "no match"
//
// A pattern that does not compile is not reported. `search` answers null and `matches`
// answers false — the same two answers a perfectly good pattern gives when the text simply
// does not contain it. So a typo in a pattern reads as *this line has no numbers in it*,
// and a scanner built on it quietly finds nothing, for every line, forever.
//
// There is no third answer to check for, so the defence is to test the pattern against text
// you know it must match — once, near where the pattern is written — rather than to guard
// each call.

fn a_pattern_that_never_compiled() {
  broken = "[unclosed";          // a character class with no `]`
  assert("abc 123".search(broken) == null, "an invalid pattern answers null…");
  assert(!"abc 123".matches(broken), "…and false");

  // …which is exactly what a VALID pattern answers when it does not match.
  assert("abc 123".search("[xyz]+") == null, "a good pattern that misses: also null");
  assert(!"abc 123".matches("[xyz]+"), "also false");

  // The check that works: assert the pattern against text it must match.
  assert("abc 123".matches("[0-9]+"), "the pattern compiles and finds what it should");

  println("invalid : answers null / false, exactly like a miss");
}

// ## The offset is a BYTE offset
//
// `search` answers where the match starts in BYTES, and `text` in loft is a UTF-8 buffer —
// so on anything but ASCII the answer is not a character index. Slice with it and compare
// it against `size()` (bytes); comparing it to `len()` (characters) reads correctly on
// ASCII and silently wrongly on everything else.
//
// A miss is `null` even though the return type says `integer`, so test the result before
// using it as a position.

fn the_offset_is_a_byte() {
  ascii = "order 1234";
  assert(ascii.search("[0-9]+") == 6, "on ASCII, bytes and characters agree");

  uni = "héllo 42";
  assert(uni.search("[0-9]+") == 7, "é is two bytes, so the digits start at byte 7…");
  assert(len(uni) == 8, "…where the CHARACTER index would be 6 of 8");
  assert(size(uni) == 9, "and the buffer is 9 bytes");

  assert(uni.search("[0-9]+") < size(uni), "compare an offset against size()");

  println("offset  : byte 7 in a 9-byte, 8-character text");
}

// ## `split_on` yields the GAPS
//
// The pieces are what lies BETWEEN matches, which has two consequences worth seeing once: a
// separator at either end leaves an empty piece, and two adjacent separators leave an empty
// piece between them. Text with no match at all comes back as one piece — the whole text.
//
// It is lazy: the iterator yields through loft's coroutine channel, so nothing is
// materialised and a `for` over a large text does not build a vector of it.

fn splitting_yields_the_gaps() {
  pieces: vector<text> = [];
  for piece in "a,b;;c".split_on("[,;]") { pieces += [piece]; }
  assert(len(pieces) == 4, "four gaps between three separators, got {len(pieces)}");
  assert(pieces[1] == "b", "the middle piece");
  assert(pieces[2] == "", "…and the empty one between the two adjacent separators");

  whole: vector<text> = [];
  for piece in "nothing here".split_on("[0-9]") { whole += [piece]; }
  assert(len(whole) == 1 and whole[0] == "nothing here", "no match: one piece, the whole text");

  println("split   : 'a,b;;c' on [,;] gives a, b, '' and c");
}

// ## The older spellings still work, and say so
//
// An earlier release shipped a pattern-first free function (`regex::find` / `regex::split`)
// and a prefixed method (`line.regex_find` / `line.regex_split`). Both still work exactly as
// they did — nothing was removed — and the compiler steers new calls to `search` /
// `split_on` so the surface converges on one spelling per operation.
//
// ⚠ `regex::find` is reachable only when QUALIFIED. A bare `find(pattern, input)` resolves
// the stdlib's `text` method first and answers a literal search — with the arguments in the
// other order, which is why it type-checks.
//
// Running this section PRINTS two `advice[superseded-call]` lines, and that is the steering
// channel working rather than a defect in the guide: the two calls below are deliberately
// the old spellings, so the compiler names the new one. Your own code sees the same line
// wherever you write them, which is how the surface converges without anything breaking.

fn the_older_spellings_still_work() {
  line = "order 1234";
  assert(regex::find("[0-9]+", line) == 6, "the qualified free function still answers");
  assert(line.regex_find("[0-9]+") == 6, "…and so does the prefixed method");
  assert(line.search("[0-9]+") == 6, "all three are the same operation");

  println("steering: find / regex_find still work, search is the one to write");
}

